{"step_index":177,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:00:46Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:00:46+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:00:46+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/prover_eval/challenge.py`\nTotal Lines: 148\nTotal Bytes: 5844\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: #!/usr/bin/env python\n2: \"\"\"tools/prover_eval/challenge.py — emit the immutable challenge pair per item.\n3: \n4: The evaluation design hands every model two declarations for one conjecture C::\n5: \n6:     theorem prove_candidate  :   C := by sorry\n7:     theorem refute_candidate : ¬ C := by sorry\n8: \n9: and counts the item resolved when either is accepted. That only measures what\n10: it claims if both mention the *same* C. In this repository they did not.\n11: \n12: The pipeline carries two Lean layers. ``GraphInvariants`` is the specification:\n13: ``sInf`` over a ``Set``, noncomputable, in the ``SimpleGraph`` namespace, so it\n14: reads ``G.zeroForcingNumber``. ``GraphInvariantsComputable`` is the executable\n15: mirror in the ``GraphCalc`` namespace, which ``decide`` can actually evaluate.\n16: The positive exporter targeted the first and the disproof exporter the second,\n17: so a \"proof\" and a \"refutation\" of one conjecture were statements about two\n18: different functions, and a model could in principle satisfy both.\n19: \n20: This module states both directions over the **computable** layer, for a reason\n21: beyond convenience: that is the layer whose agreement with graphcalc has been\n22: checked. ``tools/lean_differential.py`` verified 4,709 (graph, invariant) pairs\n23: against ``GraphCalc`` for n ≤ 6 — and caught a real defect doing it. The\n24: specification layer carries only two bridge theorems, so its agreement with the\n25: values that produced the truth labels is largely unestablished. Labelling an\n26: item with a graphcalc counterexam\n<truncated 1038 bytes>\n, or None when the statement is outside the formalized\n59:     vocabulary.\n60:     \"\"\"\n61:     from pipeline import lean_disproof as ld, linear_form\n62: \n63:     parsed = ld._parse(statement, columns)\n64:     if parsed is not None:\n65:         # `_parse` also returns the definedness hypotheses required by any\n66:         # partial invariant in the body (`minCard` has no witness on some\n67:         # graphs). They belong in the challenge for the same reason the class\n68:         # hypotheses do: without them the statement is broader than the\n69:         # conjecture and refutable on graphs it never spoke about.\n70:         classes, concl, defined = parsed\n71:         return ([f\"{ld.CLASS_PREDICATES[c]} G\" for c in classes] + defined,\n72:                 concl)\n73: \n74:     necessary = ld._parse_necessary(statement)\n75:     if necessary is not None:\n76:         hyp, concl = necessary\n77:         return [hyp], concl\n78:     return None\n79: \n80: \n81: def render_pair(statement: str, columns, ident: str):\n82:     \"\"\"(challenge_source, C_as_text) or None.\"\"\"\n83:     parts = body_for(statement, columns)\n84:     if parts is None:\n85:         return None\n86:     hyps, concl = parts\n87:     # One universally quantified proposition, written once and referenced by\n88:     # both declarations so they cannot drift apart.\n89:     arrow = \"\".join(f\"{h} → \" for h in hyps)\n90:     prop = (f\"∀ {BINDERS},\\n      {arrow}{concl}\")\n91:     src = (f\"{PREAMBLE}\\n\"\n92:            f\"-- Conjecture under test: {statement}\\n\"\n93:            f\"-- Both declarations refer to the same `Candidate`.\\n\"\n94:            f\"abbrev Candidate : Prop :=\\n    {prop}\\n\\n\"\n95:            f\"theorem prove_{ident} : Candidate := by\\n  sorry\\n\\n\"\n96:            f\"theorem refute_{ident} : ¬ Candidate := by\\n  sorry\\n\")\n97:     return src, prop\n98: \n99: \n100: def main() -> int:\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
